# rp2040_zero

A Waveshare RP2040-Zero, the mini board: an RP2040 with 2MB of flash, a W25Q16
as on the Pico, on a 23.5 by 18 mm board with a USB-C socket on the native
port and a WS2812B RGB LED on GP16.

It derives from pico because that is what it is: the RP2040 with the flash the
Pico SDK configures as pico. The SDK publishes a waveshare_rp2040_zero header,
but the bridge selects PICO_BOARD from its six vendor names, so the board
derives and the board table corrects, as every other composite board here
does.

The 23-pin header carries GP0 to GP15 and GP26 to GP29, with 3V3, GND, and
VSYS; ten castellated pads carry GP17 to GP25. GP16 drives the WS2812B's data
input and is not brought out. The keys are BOOT, on the flash's chip select,
and RESET, on RUN.

One of pico's defaults is wrong here, and the Pico bridge's board table
replaces it: GP25 is a free pad, not an LED, and the only LED is the WS2812B,
which a plain GPIO write cannot light, so the board reports no LED. pico's
default UART, UART0 on GP0 and GP1, is on the header and stands. GP26 and GP27
and GP8 and GP9 are free header pins, so the board has the second I2C and
second UART wirings the six vendor boards have. ADC_VREF is the 3V3 rail
through 200 ohms, as on the Pico, so the ADC reference is 3300 mV.

Every pin above was read from Waveshare's schematic, RP2040_Zero.pdf. None of
it is qualified on hardware.

https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/RP2040-Zero
